    "schema": "Article",
    "bluf": "Bleeding that does not match your usual pattern can show up many ways: spotting instead of a period, bleeding after sex, breakthrough bleeding on birth control, a period that starts and stops, or a period much lighter or shorter than usual. This log gives you one place to track all of it. It does not diagnose the cause or tell you what care to choose. Call a clinician now for heavy bleeding, severe pain, faintness, or bleeding after menopause.",
    "faqs": [
      {
        "q": "How many cycles should I track?",
        "a": "Start now. One good cycle is useful. A few cycles can show a clearer pattern if the issue is not urgent."
      },
      {
        "q": "Should I wait to call if bleeding feels wrong?",
        "a": "No. If bleeding is heavy, sudden, painful, or paired with faintness, shortness of breath, pregnancy concern, or bleeding after menopause, contact a clinician now."
      },
      {
        "q": "Can I track this in Floriva?",
        "a": "Yes, if you want a private app log. Floriva keeps cycle notes on your device. You can also use this page on paper."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      {
        "question": "What should I track for abnormal bleeding before a doctor visit?",
        "answer": "Track the date, cycle day, bleeding type, flow level, color, product count, clots, pain, discharge, pregnancy test result if relevant, medicines or birth control notes, and any symptoms such as dizziness or fatigue."
      },
      {
        "question": "What counts as abnormal bleeding?",
        "answer": "ACOG describes abnormal uterine bleeding as bleeding that is unusual in amount, timing, or length. This can include bleeding between periods, bleeding after sex, breakthrough bleeding on birth control, a period much lighter or shorter than usual, or bleeding after menopause."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can a bleeding log diagnose the cause?",
        "answer": "No. A log does not diagnose the cause. It helps your clinician see the pattern and decide what questions, exam, tests, or follow-up may fit your situation."
      }
    ],
    "definitions": [
      {
        "term": "Abnormal uterine bleeding",
        "definition": "Bleeding from the uterus that is unusual for you in amount, timing, regularity, or length."
      },
      {
        "term": "Spotting",
        "definition": "Light bleeding or marks that are usually too light to need a pad or tampon."
      },
      {
        "term": "Breakthrough bleeding",
        "definition": "Bleeding or spotting at a time you did not expect while using hormonal birth control."
      },
      {
        "term": "Cycle day",
        "definition": "The day number counted from the first day of full period flow."
      },
      {
        "term": "Cervicitis",
        "definition": "Irritation or swelling of the cervix. It is one possible care topic, not a self-diagnosis."
      }
    ],
