    "buyerStage": "tofu",
    "targetPersona": [
      "cycle-syncing-curious",
      "active-cycle-tracker",
      "performance-optimizer",
      "doctor-prep"
    ],
    "schema": "Article",
    "bluf": "Cycle syncing means adjusting exercise, food, and work demands across the four cycle phases. Some of the science is solid; some is marketing. This planner covers the better-evidenced phase patterns, a monthly planning grid, symptom logs for exercise during your period, a log for exercise and weight changes tied to a late period, and a food-and-symptom diary. It does not prescribe workouts, diets, or treatment.",
    "faqs": [
      {
        "q": "Does this planner tell me what to eat or how hard to train?",
        "a": "No. It gives phase-based patterns backed by research and space to track your own experience. It does not prescribe workouts, diets, or supplements."
      },
      {
        "q": "What if my cycle is irregular?",
        "a": "Fill in the phase reference first, then use estimated phase windows based on recent cycle history. If your luteal phase is fairly consistent, anchor it backward from your expected period date. Patterns usually become visible after two to three months."
      },
      {
        "q": "Can this planner tell me why my period is late?",
        "a": "No. It helps you build a plain timeline of exercise, food, sleep, stress, and weight changes to bring to a clinician. It does not diagnose the cause or tell you if you are pregnant."
      },
      {
        "q": "Can Floriva hold these notes?",
        "a": "Yes. Floriva can keep short cycle and symptom notes on your device. You can skip gym names, exact routes, times, or weight and still keep a useful record."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      {
        "question": "What is cycle syncing and does the research support it?",
        "answer": "Cycle syncing means adjusting exercise, food, and work demands across the four menstrual cycle phases to match the hormonal environment of each phase. The evidence is uneven: there is solid support for estrogen affecting strength and endurance, progesterone raising resting core temperature in the luteal phase, and better insulin sensitivity in the follicular phase. Claims about specific foods for each phase or precise day-by-day mood predictions are weakly supported."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can exercise make a period late?",
        "answer": "It can be one factor for some people. The Office on Women's Health says too much exercise can cause missed periods or make periods stop, and that irregular or missed periods are more common in athletes and people who train hard often. A clinician can help check what applies to you."
      },
      {
        "question": "What should I track before and after exercising during my period?",
        "answer": "Track the date, period day, planned and actual movement, sleep, energy, cramps, flow, leak worry, dizziness, and fatigue before, and cramps, flow or leak issues, and dizziness after. Short notes are enough."
      },
      {
        "question": "How much should I change my routine based on cycle phase?",
        "answer": "That depends on how sensitive you are to hormonal changes and what you want to optimize. This planner is a tool for noticing patterns, not a prescription -- track what you actually did alongside your phase and let your own data guide adjustments over two to three cycles."
      }
    ],
    "definitions": [
      {
        "term": "Luteal phase",
        "definition": "The second half of the menstrual cycle, from ovulation to the start of the next period, typically 10 to 16 days. Progesterone rises then falls, raising resting body temperature and often increasing carbohydrate use during exercise."
      },
      {
        "term": "Follicular phase",
        "definition": "The first half of the menstrual cycle, from the first day of menstruation through ovulation. Rising estrogen is linked to improved insulin sensitivity and better recovery from strength training in research settings."
      },
      {
        "term": "Training load",
        "definition": "A plain note about how much exercise changed, such as more days, longer sessions, harder effort, a new sport, or less rest."
      },
      {
        "term": "Lower-effort option",
        "definition": "A softer movement plan you can choose instead of your usual plan on a day your body needs less."
      },
      {
        "term": "Food access",
        "definition": "Whether you had enough food, regular meals, money for food, time to eat, or safe access to food -- one possible factor in irregular or missed periods."
      }
    ],
