    "schema": "Article",
    "bluf": "PCOS can involve irregular periods, acne, excess hair growth, hair thinning, insulin resistance, and fertility concerns. This kit gives you one place to track cycles and symptoms, acne and hair changes, lab results, medicine and supplement changes, and meals, plus questions for your next visit. It cannot diagnose PCOS or tell you what treatment to use.",
    "faqs": [
      {
        "q": "Is this kit medical advice?",
        "a": "No. It is a set of tracking sheets and question lists. It cannot diagnose PCOS, rule it out, or choose treatment."
      },
      {
        "q": "Is the meal section a prescribed diet plan?",
        "a": "No. It is a planning worksheet. It does not prescribe calories or macros, ban food groups, or promise weight loss or cycle changes."
      },
      {
        "q": "Can Floriva diagnose PCOS?",
        "a": "No. Floriva is a tracker. It can help you keep notes on your device. It does not diagnose, treat, or interpret labs."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      {
        "question": "What symptoms can PCOS cause?",
        "answer": "PCOS can involve irregular periods, missed periods, acne, excess facial or body hair, hair thinning, insulin resistance signs, and fertility concerns. Symptoms vary between people."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can a symptom tracker diagnose PCOS?",
        "answer": "No. A tracker cannot diagnose PCOS. A clinician may use irregular ovulation, signs or levels of androgens, and ovarian findings, along with your history and exam, to decide what fits."
      },
      {
        "question": "What should I track for PCOS?",
        "answer": "Track period start dates and cycle length, acne and hair changes, lab results, medicines and supplements, and meals and energy patterns. Three cycles of tracking gives a clearer pattern than one snapshot."
      }
    ],
    "definitions": [
      {
        "term": "PCOS",
        "definition": "Polycystic ovary syndrome. A hormone condition that can affect periods, ovulation, skin, hair, and metabolic health."
      },
      {
        "term": "Androgen",
        "definition": "A hormone group that includes testosterone. Higher androgen activity can be linked with acne or increased hair growth, but photos and symptoms alone cannot measure hormone levels."
      },
      {
        "term": "Hirsutism",
        "definition": "Increased hair growth in areas such as the face, chest, belly, or upper thighs."
      },
      {
        "term": "Reference range",
        "definition": "The range a lab prints next to a result. It is a comparison range, not a diagnosis."
      },
      {
        "term": "Insulin resistance",
        "definition": "A state where the body does not respond to insulin as well as expected. It is common in PCOS, but a clinician should test and explain it."
      }
    ],
