    "schema": "Article",
    "bluf": "This tracker helps you log perimenopause bleeding, hot flashes, sleep, mood, brain fog, and body changes so you can see patterns over time. It groups symptoms so long lists feel less overwhelming. It does not diagnose perimenopause, rule out other causes, or tell you what treatment to use.",
    "faqs": [
      {
        "q": "Is this medical advice?",
        "a": "No. It is a tracking and question-prep tool. Ask a clinician about bleeding changes, severe symptoms, or anything that worries you."
      },
      {
        "q": "How long should I track?",
        "a": "Try 4 to 8 weeks, or once a week for 1 to 3 months if daily tracking feels like too much. Keep going if symptoms are changing or you are waiting for a visit."
      },
      {
        "q": "Can Floriva help with this?",
        "a": "Floriva can help you keep cycle and symptom notes on your device. You still choose what private details to type or share."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      {
        "question": "What should I track during perimenopause?",
        "answer": "Track bleeding pattern, hot flashes, night sweats, sleep, mood, headaches, brain fog, vaginal dryness, medicines, and how much each symptom affects daily life."
      },
      {
        "question": "Is there an official list of 34 or 100 perimenopause symptoms?",
        "answer": "No. Major medical sources do not use one official symptom count. Office on Women's Health groups changes into categories: bleeding, hot flashes, night sweats, sleep problems, mood changes, vaginal and urinary changes, and body changes."
      },
      {
        "question": "When should perimenopause bleeding be checked by a clinician?",
        "answer": "ACOG says to talk with an ob-gyn about bleeding changes near menopause, especially bleeding that is much heavier than usual, lasts longer than 7 days, happens between periods, happens after sex, or happens after 12 months with no period. Bleeding after menopause should always be checked."
      }
    ],
    "definitions": [
      {
        "term": "Perimenopause",
        "definition": "The transition before menopause. Periods and symptoms can change during this time, and it can last 2 to 8 years."
      },
      {
        "term": "Menopause",
        "definition": "The point reached after 12 months in a row with no bleeding or spotting."
      },
      {
        "term": "Hot flash",
        "definition": "A sudden warm feeling that may cause sweating, flushing, chills, or a racing heart."
      },
      {
        "term": "Night sweat",
        "definition": "A hot flash during sleep that causes sweating."
      },
      {
        "term": "Brain fog",
        "definition": "Trouble finding words, focusing, or remembering things."
      }
    ],
