    "schema": "Article",
    "bluf": "PMDD cannot be confirmed from memory alone. A clinician usually needs daily symptom ratings across at least 2 cycles, plus a plan for safety, treatment, work, and relationships. This kit gives you all of it in one place: a daily tracker, an appointment checklist, a safety plan, a treatment response log, and scripts for support people and for work or school. If you have thoughts of self-harm, feel unsafe, or might hurt someone, seek urgent help now.",
    "faqs": [
      {
        "q": "Can this kit diagnose PMDD?",
        "a": "No. It helps you collect the daily record a clinician may need. A clinician still has to review the pattern and rule out other causes."
      },
      {
        "q": "Do I have to use every part of this kit?",
        "a": "No. Use the daily tracker first. Add the safety plan, treatment tracker, or scripts only if they fit your situation."
      },
      {
        "q": "Can Floriva recommend PMDD treatment?",
        "a": "No. Floriva can help you track cycle and symptom notes. It does not recommend treatment. A clinician should review your pattern and safety needs."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      {
        "question": "How many cycles should I track for PMDD?",
        "answer": "Track daily symptoms for at least 2 cycles. ACOG and clinical diagnostic guidance rely on prospective daily ratings because memory alone can miss the timing and severity of symptoms."
      },
      {
        "question": "What should a full PMDD tracking kit include?",
        "answer": "A useful kit includes a daily mood and body symptom log across two cycles, a safety plan, an appointment prep checklist, a treatment response tracker, and scripts for talking with a partner, family member, or workplace."
      },
      {
        "question": "What makes PMDD different from PMS?",
        "answer": "PMDD causes stronger mood symptoms and more disruption than PMS. Timing matters too. PMDD symptoms rise before a period and improve within a few days after bleeding starts."
      },
      {
        "question": "When should I get urgent help?",
        "answer": "Get urgent help now if you may hurt yourself, may hurt someone else, feel unsafe, or cannot get through the day safely. Use emergency services, a crisis line, or urgent care."
      }
    ],
