    "schema": "Article",
    "bluf": "Premenstrual symptoms can hit mood, sleep, digestion, and the body all at once. This tracker covers all of it in one place, sorted into four groups: mood, energy and sleep, digestive, and physical. It does not diagnose PMS, PMDD, or any other condition. Use it to bring clear notes and questions to a clinician.",
    "faqs": [
      {
        "q": "How many cycles should I track?",
        "a": "Start with one cycle. Two or three cycles make the pattern easier to see if the issue is not urgent or unsafe."
      },
      {
        "q": "Should I share every note with a clinician?",
        "a": "No. Share the smallest useful summary. Each group below ends with a one-page summary you can use instead of the full log."
      },
      {
        "q": "Can Floriva hold this tracker?",
        "a": "Yes. Floriva can keep short cycle and symptom notes on your device. Paper works too."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      {
        "question": "What counts as a premenstrual symptom?",
        "answer": "ACOG, the Office on Women's Health, and Mayo Clinic list mood changes, fatigue, sleep trouble, bloating, cramps, breast tenderness, headache, food cravings, and bowel changes among possible PMS symptoms."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do I know if my symptoms are PMS or something else?",
        "answer": "Timing is the main clue clinicians look for. PMS and PMDD symptoms tend to rise before a period and ease within a few days after bleeding starts. A tracker can show that pattern, but only a clinician can review it against other causes."
      },
      {
        "question": "Do I need to track all four groups?",
        "answer": "No. Use the groups that fit you. Many people only need one or two sections. The mood section includes a safety check that everyone should read first."
      }
    ],
    "definitions": [
      {
        "term": "Cycle day",
        "definition": "The day number counted from the first day of full period flow."
      },
      {
        "term": "Luteal phase",
        "definition": "The part of the cycle after ovulation and before the next period, when premenstrual symptoms often rise."
      },
      {
        "term": "Daily impact",
        "definition": "How a symptom changed work, school, sleep, care tasks, relationships, or safety."
      },
      {
        "term": "Safety note",
        "definition": "A short note about whether you felt safe, felt out of control, or needed immediate help."
      }
    ],
