    "steps": [
      {
        "title": "If you want notes before your appointment",
        "content": "One period can still be useful, but more than one cycle can make patterns easier to discuss. If you have an appointment scheduled, you can use Floriva to note the basics from your next period. Incomplete notes are better than trying to remember everything later."
      },
      {
        "title": "Log the things doctors actually ask about",
        "content": "In Floriva, you can note period start date, period end date, flow level, spotting between periods, and the symptoms you most want to discuss. Do not log everything if it means logging nothing. A short, steady record is usually easier to use than a long record with many gaps."
      },
      {
        "title": "Write a notes entry for anything unusual",
        "content": "Floriva's notes field is where short context goes: the period that was much heavier than usual, mid-cycle pain that lasted three days, a week on antibiotics, or spotting after sex. Date each note and write enough context that it makes sense later."
      },
      {
        "title": "Make a short summary before the appointment",
        "content": "If useful, review your Floriva records and make a short summary. You can include cycle length range, period duration, cycles that were different, symptoms that repeat, and events worth asking about, such as a missed period, spotting, or unusual pain."
      },
      {
        "title": "Bring the summary, not the app",
        "content": "A one-page handwritten or printed summary is often easier to discuss than scrolling through an app. Format it as: date range covered, cycle lengths, period details, key symptoms, and questions you want to ask."
      }
    ],
