    "bluf": "Period bloating can show up before bleeding or during period days. Timing can help you explain the pattern, but it does not prove the cause. Track bloating, gas, stool changes, pain, and red-flag symptoms so you can bring clearer notes to care.",
    "faqs": [
      {
        "q": "Can tracking bloating help identify food triggers?",
        "a": "Tracking can help you compare bloating, stool changes, food notes, and cycle day. It cannot prove the cause, but it can make a visit easier."
      },
      {
        "q": "Is severe period bloating a sign of something more serious?",
        "a": "Bloating that is severe, new, worsening, or paired with pelvic pain, bowel changes, blood in stool, fever, vomiting, weight loss, or pregnancy concern should be discussed with a clinician."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      {
        "question": "Why does bloating happen before your period?",
        "answer": "Some people notice bloating before a period. OWH and Mayo Clinic list bloating and digestive changes as possible PMS symptoms. Timing can help, but it does not prove the cause for one person."
      },
      {
        "question": "When does period bloating go away?",
        "answer": "Some people feel better after bleeding starts. If bloating is severe, new, lasts outside period timing, or comes with pain, blood in stool, fever, vomiting, weight loss, pregnancy concern, or symptoms that feel unsafe, ask a clinician."
      }
    ],
    "definitions": [
      {
        "term": "Luteal phase",
        "definition": "The second half of the menstrual cycle, from ovulation to the start of the next period."
      },
      {
        "term": "Progesterone",
        "definition": "A hormone made after ovulation. It is one part of the cycle pattern that some people notice before a period."
      }
    ],
