    "schema": "Article",
    "bluf": "Thyroid hormones directly regulate the menstrual cycle. Hypothyroidism tends to produce heavier, longer, more frequent periods, while hyperthyroidism tends to produce lighter, shorter, or absent periods. Tracking cycle changes alongside thyroid symptoms creates the clinical connection that leads to testing.",
    "faqs": [
      {
        "q": "Should I track my thyroid symptoms alongside my cycle?",
        "a": "Yes. Noting energy levels, temperature sensitivity, hair changes, and weight fluctuations alongside your cycle data helps your provider connect menstrual irregularities to a possible thyroid cause. Data across multiple cycles is more useful than a single month."
      },
      {
        "q": "How quickly do periods normalize after thyroid treatment?",
        "a": "It varies. Some people see cycle improvement within one to two months of reaching appropriate thyroid hormone levels. Others take three to six months. Tracking your cycle through treatment helps you and your provider assess whether the medication dose is adequate."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      {
        "question": "How does hypothyroidism affect your period?",
        "answer": "Hypothyroidism tends to increase menstrual flow and frequency. Low thyroid hormone levels can impair ovulation, alter the balance of estrogen and progesterone, and affect how the uterine lining sheds. Periods may become heavier, longer, and closer together. Some people with significant hypothyroidism stop ovulating, which can lead to irregular or absent periods despite the general trend toward heavier bleeding."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can thyroid problems cause missed periods?",
        "answer": "Yes. Both hypothyroidism and hyperthyroidism can cause missed periods. Hyperthyroidism more commonly leads to lighter or absent periods. Severe hypothyroidism can also stop ovulation entirely, leading to missed cycles. If you have unexplained missed periods alongside fatigue, weight changes, or temperature sensitivity, thyroid testing is reasonable to request."
      }
    ],
    "definitions": [
      {
        "term": "Hypothyroidism",
        "definition": "A condition where the thyroid gland produces insufficient thyroid hormone, slowing metabolism. Common symptoms include fatigue, weight gain, cold intolerance, and menstrual changes including heavier or more frequent periods."
      },
      {
        "term": "Hyperthyroidism",
        "definition": "A condition where the thyroid gland produces excess thyroid hormone, accelerating metabolism. Symptoms include anxiety, weight loss, heat intolerance, rapid heart rate, and menstrual changes including lighter or absent periods."
      },
      {
        "term": "TSH (thyroid-stimulating hormone)",
        "definition": "A hormone produced by the pituitary gland that signals the thyroid to produce hormones. High TSH suggests hypothyroidism (the pituitary is working harder to stimulate an underactive thyroid). Low TSH suggests hyperthyroidism."
      }
    ],
