    "title": "Floriva vs Stardust: Features, Privacy, and Data Compared",
    "description": "Stardust markets itself as privacy-focused after Dobbs. Floriva is local-first with no readable central records. Here's an honest comparison of what each app does and what 'privacy-focused' actually means in each case.",
    "tags": [
      "Stardust",
      "Floriva",
      "comparison",
      "privacy",
      "period tracker",
      "data sharing"
    ],
    "publishedAt": "2026-04-29",
    "updatedAt": "2026-04-29",
    "buyerStage": "mofu",
    "targetPersona": [
      "privacy-conscious",
      "data-sensitive"
    ],
    "bluf": "Stardust surged in downloads after the Dobbs decision, marketing itself as the privacy alternative to Flo. An independent analysis found Stardust's app contained third-party tracking SDKs despite its privacy marketing, raising questions about the gap between stated policy and actual data collection. Floriva's privacy approach is architectural, no readable central records, not marketing-based. The comparison is less about features than about what 'private' actually means.",
    "faqs": [],
    "answers": [
      {
        "question": "Is Stardust actually private",
        "answer": "Stardust's privacy claims were scrutinized after the Dobbs decision. The app's marketing positioned it as a private period tracker, but 2022 analyses by tech press (Vice/Motherboard reporting by Joseph Cox) and independent privacy researchers found the app contained third-party SDKs including an advertising attribution tracker (AppsFlyer) at the time. Stardust subsequently updated its app and privacy practices. Whether current Stardust versions maintain or have removed these trackers is worth independently verifying. Use Exodus Privacy to check current SDK presence."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is the difference between Floriva and Stardust",
        "answer": "The core difference is architectural. Stardust is a cloud-based app. Data is stored on servers, accounts are required, and despite privacy marketing, the fundamental infrastructure creates the same legal exposure as other cloud apps. Floriva stores data locally with no readable central records. The privacy differences that matter in practice (subpoena exposure, breach exposure, data broker risk) are driven by architecture, not by marketing positioning."
      },
      {
        "question": "How can I check if Stardust has tracking SDKs",
        "answer": "For Android: Exodus Privacy (exodus-privacy.eu.org) catalogs SDKs detected in Android apps. Search for 'Stardust' in their database to see current SDK analysis. Note that Exodus analyzes specific APK versions. Check the date of the analysis and compare to the current app version. For iOS: use a network analyzer like mitmproxy or Charles Proxy to monitor what traffic the app sends; this requires technical setup. App Store privacy labels are self-reported and may not list specific SDKs."
      },
      {
        "question": "Which app is better for cycle tracking features",
        "answer": "Both apps cover period date logging, symptom tracking, and cycle prediction. Stardust has social features and a more polished consumer UI. Floriva focuses on local-first cycle notes and pattern review for users who want less company-held data. Feature preference depends on what you're tracking for: social community vs. private cycle review."
      }
    ],
    "definitions": [
      {
        "term": "SDK audit",
        "definition": "Analysis of an app's compiled code to identify embedded third-party libraries (SDKs). Tools like Exodus Privacy do this automatically for Android apps by decompiling APKs. An SDK audit reveals what third-party services the app sends data to, which is often not disclosed in privacy policies. SDKs can include advertising networks, analytics platforms, crash reporters, and attribution trackers, each with their own data collection behavior."
      },
      {
        "term": "Privacy marketing vs. privacy architecture",
        "definition": "Privacy marketing refers to how a company positions its privacy practices in its branding and statements. Privacy architecture refers to how data actually flows in the technical system. A company can have strong privacy marketing with weak architecture (cloud storage, SDK trackers, required accounts) or minimal marketing with strong architecture (local-only storage, no accounts, no SDKs). The Stardust controversy illustrated the gap: the marketing claimed privacy while the architecture included trackers. Architecture is verifiable; marketing is not."
      }
    ],
