    "title": "The Fertile Window Explained: When You Can Actually Get Pregnant",
    "description": "The fertile window is the 5-6 days each cycle when pregnancy is possible, 5 days before ovulation plus ovulation day. Day 14 is a myth for most people. Here's how to find yours.",
    "tags": [
      "fertile window",
      "ovulation",
      "fertility",
      "cycle tracking",
      "conception",
      "TTC"
    ],
    "publishedAt": "2026-04-29",
    "updatedAt": "2026-04-29",
    "buyerStage": "tofu",
    "targetPersona": [
      "health-tracker",
      "privacy-conscious"
    ],
    "bluf": "The fertile window is the 5-6 days each cycle when conception is possible: the 5 days before ovulation (sperm can survive up to 5 days in fertile cervical mucus) plus ovulation day itself (the egg is viable for 12-24 hours). The window's timing shifts with ovulation timing, which varies cycle to cycle in most people. The standard 'Day 14' assumption is accurate only for textbook 28-day cycles with ovulation on Day 14, which describes fewer than 30% of real cycles.",
    "faqs": [],
    "answers": [
      {
        "question": "What is the fertile window?",
        "answer": "The fertile window is the 5 to 6 days each menstrual cycle when pregnancy is biologically possible. It spans the 5 days before ovulation plus ovulation day itself. Sperm can survive in fertile cervical mucus (the egg-white consistency) for up to 5 days. The egg is viable for 12 to 24 hours after release. Outside this window, the sperm have died or the egg has broken down, making conception extremely unlikely."
      },
      {
        "question": "When is the fertile window in a 28-day cycle?",
        "answer": "In a textbook 28-day cycle with ovulation on Day 14, the fertile window falls roughly on Days 10 to 15. But 'textbook' is misleading. Studies tracking actual ovulation show that ovulation on exactly Day 14 is uncommon even in people with 28-day cycles. Day 14 is a statistical average, not a reliable personal prediction. The only way to accurately find your fertile window is to track ovulation directly with LH strips or BBT."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do I find my fertile window?",
        "answer": "The most reliable approach uses two signals together: LH test strips (which detect the LH surge 24 to 36 hours before ovulation) and cervical mucus observation (egg-white consistency peaks in the days before ovulation). LH strips predict ovulation. Cervical mucus extends the picture by showing when sperm can survive in the reproductive tract. BBT charting confirms ovulation after it has already occurred, which is useful for pattern analysis but too late for the current cycle."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can the fertile window change each cycle?",
        "answer": "Yes, significantly. The fertile window shifts with ovulation timing, which varies cycle to cycle in response to stress, illness, travel, sleep disruption, and other factors. A 2003 study of 213 women found that only 30% had their fertile window fall entirely within Days 10 to 17, the commonly cited 'safe' tracking range. Relying on a fixed calendar window is the main reason for both unintended pregnancies and missed conception attempts."
      }
    ],
    "definitions": [
      {
        "term": "Ovulation",
        "definition": "The release of a mature egg from a dominant ovarian follicle, triggered by the LH surge. Ovulation is a single event, not a period. It takes approximately 10-12 minutes from follicle rupture to egg release. The egg is viable for 12-24 hours post-release; if not fertilized within this window, it degenerates. The LH surge precedes ovulation by 24-36 hours, making a positive LH test the most accessible predictor of the fertile window."
      },
      {
        "term": "Sperm viability in fertile mucus",
        "definition": "Sperm can survive for 3-5 days in fertile-quality cervical mucus, the thin, slippery, egg-white consistency produced under high estrogen in the days before ovulation. Outside this window, cervical mucus is thick and hostile to sperm, dramatically reducing viability to hours. This is why sex on the days before ovulation, not just on ovulation day itself, accounts for a significant proportion of conceptions."
      }
    ],
