    "title": "How to Test Hormones at Home: What Works, What Doesn't, and What Timing Matters",
    "description": "At-home hormone tests for LH, FSH, progesterone, and thyroid: which have real clinical value, what timing requirements make or break the result, and when to use a lab instead.",
    "tags": [
      "hormone testing",
      "at-home tests",
      "ovulation tests",
      "progesterone",
      "cycle tracking"
    ],
    "publishedAt": "2026-04-29",
    "updatedAt": "2026-04-29",
    "buyerStage": "mofu",
    "targetPersona": [
      "health-tracker",
      "privacy-conscious",
      "data-sensitive"
    ],
    "bluf": "LH test strips (ovulation predictor kits) are validated, inexpensive, and useful for detecting the ovulation surge. Progesterone blood spot tests require correct cycle day timing to mean anything. An untimed draw is often misleading. FSH home tests can flag elevated levels but require context (cycle day, symptoms, age) to interpret. Saliva hormone tests are marketed aggressively but are inconsistently correlated with serum levels. No at-home test replaces a serum draw for clinical decision-making.",
    "faqs": [],
    "answers": [
      {
        "question": "Are at-home hormone tests accurate?",
        "answer": "It depends on the test. LH urine strips (OPKs) are highly reliable for detecting the LH surge. They are validated against serum LH and form the basis of standard ovulation prediction. Progesterone blood spot tests are reasonably accurate if drawn on the correct cycle day. FSH home tests (urine-based) detect elevated FSH but provide no reference range without context. Saliva tests for estrogen, cortisol, and progesterone are inconsistently correlated with serum levels and are not clinically recommended for most hormone evaluations."
      },
      {
        "question": "When should I test progesterone at home?",
        "answer": "For a progesterone blood spot test to be meaningful, it must be drawn 7 days after confirmed ovulation. In a typical 28-day cycle, this is Day 21. That is why it is called the Day 21 progesterone test. If your cycle is longer or shorter, calculate 7 days post-ovulation using a confirmed LH surge or BBT shift. An untimed draw is nearly uninterpretable because progesterone changes from near-zero at menstruation to 10 or more ng/mL at mid-luteal."
      },
      {
        "question": "What does a positive LH test actually mean?",
        "answer": "A positive LH test (two lines of equal darkness, or a peak on a digital OPK) indicates an LH surge has begun. Ovulation typically follows 24 to 36 hours later. A positive LH test does not confirm ovulation. It confirms the surge that normally triggers ovulation. In PCOS, multiple false surges can occur without ovulation. BBT confirmation (a sustained temperature rise) is needed to confirm ovulation actually happened."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can I test estrogen at home?",
        "answer": "Estrogen home tests exist (urine E3G tests are used in some connected fertility monitors) but provide relative values rather than absolute serum estradiol levels. The most widely available home estrogen indicator is the cervical mucus pattern. Egg-white cervical mucus correlates with the estrogen peak. For absolute estradiol levels, a serum blood draw on cycle Day 3 is the standard clinical measurement."
      }
    ],
    "definitions": [
      {
        "term": "LH (luteinizing hormone)",
        "definition": "A pituitary hormone that triggers ovulation. The LH surge, a rapid rise over 24 to 36 hours, is detected by urine OPKs and typically precedes egg release by 24 to 36 hours. LH is also elevated persistently in some people with PCOS (without producing a surge), which can cause false-positive OPK readings."
      },
      {
        "term": "Blood spot testing",
        "definition": "A hormone testing method in which a small blood sample is collected by finger prick on a filter paper card and mailed to a laboratory. The dried blood spot is then analyzed for hormone levels. Accuracy is generally good for progesterone when timing is correct. Most commercial at-home hormone tests are blood spot tests rather than lateral flow strips."
      },
      {
        "term": "FSH (follicle-stimulating hormone)",
        "definition": "A pituitary hormone that drives follicle development. Elevated FSH on cycle Day 3 indicates declining ovarian reserve or ovarian insufficiency. The pituitary compensates for reduced ovarian response by sending stronger signals. The threshold for concern varies by age. FSH above 10 mIU/mL on Day 3 warrants discussion with a provider."
      }
    ],
