    "bluf": "Seed cycling alternates flaxseed and pumpkin seed in the follicular phase with sesame and sunflower seed in the luteal phase. The proposed mechanism, lignans from flax support estrogen metabolism; zinc from pumpkin supports progesterone production; lignans from sesame support progesterone in the luteal phase, has biochemical plausibility. What it does not have is randomized controlled trial evidence. The practice is low-cost and nutritionally beneficial regardless of its hormonal effects. Its greatest value may be the attention to cycle phases it encourages, not the seeds themselves.",
    "faqs": [],
    "answers": [
      {
        "question": "Does seed cycling actually work?",
        "answer": "There is one small pilot study (2021, 30 participants) showing some cycle length improvements in people with irregular cycles. No RCT has been conducted. The proposed mechanisms, lignan-mediated estrogen metabolism support and zinc-mediated progesterone support, are plausible but unproven in this specific format. Seed cycling is unlikely to cause harm and provides genuine nutritional benefits. It should not replace medical evaluation for hormonal conditions."
      },
      {
        "question": "What seeds do you eat for which phase?",
        "answer": "Follicular phase (Day 1 through ovulation): 1 tablespoon each of ground flaxseed and pumpkin seed per day. Luteal phase (ovulation through end of cycle): 1 tablespoon each of sesame seed and raw sunflower seed per day. Seeds should be ground (for flax and sesame) or raw and unroasted to preserve the active compounds."
      },
      {
        "question": "Why does seed cycling alternate seeds?",
        "answer": "The proposed rationale: flaxseed lignans modulate estrogen by competing for estrogen receptor binding, which may reduce estrogenic excess in the follicular phase. Pumpkin seeds are high in zinc, which supports follicle development and corpus luteum function. After ovulation, sesame seed lignans may help maintain progesterone levels, and sunflower seeds provide vitamin E, which has antioxidant effects on the corpus luteum."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can seed cycling regulate an irregular period?",
        "answer": "The 2021 pilot study mentioned above found some improvement in cycle regularity in people with irregular cycles. The study was small and uncontrolled, meaning the improvement could be due to other factors or regression to the mean. Seed cycling is unlikely to regularize cycles disrupted by PCOS, thyroid disease, or hypothalamic amenorrhea without addressing the underlying cause."
      }
    ],
    "definitions": [
      {
        "term": "Lignans",
        "definition": "Polyphenolic compounds found in flaxseed, sesame, rye, and certain vegetables. In the gut, lignans are converted by bacteria to enterolignans (enterodiol and enterolactone), which are classified as phytoestrogens. They can weakly bind to estrogen receptors. Unlike strong phytoestrogens, enterolignans have a modulating effect that may reduce estrogenic excess in high-estrogen states while providing mild estrogenic support in low-estrogen states."
      },
      {
        "term": "Phytoestrogen",
        "definition": "A plant-derived compound that binds to estrogen receptors with weaker affinity than endogenous estrogen. Phytoestrogens can have estrogenic or anti-estrogenic effects depending on the hormonal context. In high-estrogen states they may competitively reduce estrogen receptor activity; in low-estrogen states they may provide mild estrogenic stimulation."
      }
    ],
