    "title": "Cervical Mucus Throughout the Cycle: Types, Changes, and What They Mean",
    "description": "Cervical mucus changes from dry to sticky to creamy to egg-white across the menstrual cycle. Here's what each type looks like, what it signals hormonally, and how to distinguish it from infection.",
    "tags": [
      "cervical mucus",
      "fertility",
      "cycle tracking",
      "ovulation",
      "vaginal discharge"
    ],
    "publishedAt": "2026-04-29",
    "updatedAt": "2026-04-29",
    "buyerStage": "tofu",
    "targetPersona": [
      "health-tracker",
      "privacy-conscious"
    ],
    "bluf": "Cervical mucus is produced by the cervix in response to estrogen and progesterone, and changes predictably throughout the cycle. The four primary types (dry, sticky, creamy, and egg-white) correspond to different hormonal phases. Egg-white cervical mucus (clear, slippery, stretchy) marks peak estrogen and the 1-3 days before ovulation. After ovulation, progesterone causes mucus to thicken and become non-fertile. This pattern is the biological basis of the Billings Ovulation Method.",
    "faqs": [],
    "answers": [
      {
        "question": "What does cervical mucus look like throughout the cycle?",
        "answer": "Immediately after menstruation: dry or minimal. Early follicular: sticky, white or yellow-white, doesn't stretch. Mid-follicular: creamy, lotion-like, slight stretch. Pre-ovulatory: egg-white, clear, slippery, stretchy (2-5cm without breaking). Post-ovulatory: returns to sticky or dry quickly. The transition from creamy to egg-white is the fertility window opening; the return to sticky after egg-white is the fertility window closing."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do you check cervical mucus?",
        "answer": "Check at the vaginal opening (not internally) using clean fingertips or toilet paper, before using the toilet (water alters the mucus), and before any sexual activity. Collect a small amount and stretch it between thumb and forefinger. Note the color, consistency (stretchy or not), and whether it's slippery. Check daily and log the result using a simple notation (dry, sticky, creamy, egg-white). Consistency of observation time and method improves pattern recognition."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can cervical mucus predict ovulation?",
        "answer": "Egg-white cervical mucus predicts that ovulation is within 1-3 days. The peak day (last day of egg-white mucus) closely corresponds to ovulation day in most cycles. However, mucus prediction is not identical to LH surge detection. Mucus reflects estrogen level (which peaks before the LH surge), while LH strips detect the actual ovulatory trigger. The two used together provide better accuracy than either alone."
      },
      {
        "question": "What's the difference between cervical mucus and other vaginal discharge?",
        "answer": "Cervical mucus changes predictably across the cycle in response to estrogen and progesterone. Infection-related discharge does not follow this pattern. BV produces gray-white, watery discharge with fishy odor. Yeast produces thick, white, clumpy discharge with itching. Normal vaginal discharge between cycles varies in amount but is typically clear to white without odor or discomfort. Fertile cervical mucus is specifically clear-to-white, slippery, and stretchy, not clumpy or odorous."
      }
    ],
    "definitions": [
      {
        "term": "Billings Ovulation Method",
        "definition": "A fertility awareness method developed by Drs. John and Evelyn Billings in the 1950s and 1960s that uses cervical mucus observation exclusively to identify the fertile window. Practitioners record daily mucus quality and use the peak day (last day of lubricative mucus) plus a 3-day post-peak drying phase to identify the post-fertile period. Approved by the WHO as a family planning method; effective with correct use and instruction."
      },
      {
        "term": "Glycoprotein fibers",
        "definition": "The structural components of cervical mucus. Non-fertile mucus has randomly arranged glycoprotein fibers that form a dense mesh, blocking sperm passage. Fertile mucus (under high estrogen) has aligned parallel fibers that form channels, facilitating sperm transport, survival, and capacitation. This structural difference explains why fertile mucus literally enables conception while non-fertile mucus prevents it."
      }
    ],
