    "bluf": "Exercise during menstruation is safe for most people and often reduces cramping through endorphin release and improved uterine blood flow. Light-to-moderate activity is consistently beneficial; heavy exercise when iron-depleted from blood loss amplifies fatigue. The main exceptions are severe endometriosis or adenomyosis, where high-intensity exercise may increase retrograde flow-related pain. Rest is legitimate if you're fatigued - but it's not medically required.",
    "faqs": [],
    "answers": [
      {
        "question": "Is it okay to exercise when you have your period",
        "answer": "Yes. Exercise during menstruation is safe and for many people improves symptoms. Endorphins released during exercise reduce prostaglandin-mediated pain, which is the mechanism causing most period cramps. A 20-30 minute walk or light jog during crampy days often provides meaningful relief. There is no physiological reason to avoid exercise during a period in the absence of underlying conditions."
      },
      {
        "question": "Does exercise make period cramps worse",
        "answer": "Light-to-moderate exercise typically reduces cramps - endorphins are natural COX inhibitors and analgesics. High-intensity exercise may temporarily increase flow in some people by increasing prostaglandin production, and very intense sessions when iron-depleted can amplify fatigue. People with endometriosis or adenomyosis sometimes find high-intensity exercise worsens pelvic pain, likely due to increased retrograde flow and inflammation from intense physical stress."
      },
      {
        "question": "What exercises are good during your period",
        "answer": "The best exercises during menstruation are whatever you have energy for - walking, yoga, swimming, light cycling, and gentle strength training all work well. There is no exercise type that is categorically harmful during a period. Yoga poses that decompress the lower abdomen (child's pose, cat-cow) provide comfort during cramping. Avoid very intense workouts if you're significantly fatigued from blood loss."
      },
      {
        "question": "Should you rest during your period",
        "answer": "Rest is legitimate if you're genuinely fatigued - prostaglandins cause systemic effects including fatigue, and iron loss from heavy flow contributes. You don't need a medical reason to rest. But 'rest during your period' as a rule rather than a response to actual symptoms isn't physiologically necessary for most people. Paying attention to your actual energy level rather than following a rule is the more useful approach."
      }
    ],
    "definitions": [
      {
        "term": "Endorphins",
        "definition": "Neuropeptides released during exercise that act as natural analgesics - they bind opioid receptors and reduce pain perception. Endorphins also modulate prostaglandin signaling, which is why aerobic exercise during menstruation often reduces cramping rather than worsening it. Even 20 minutes of moderate-intensity aerobic activity is sufficient to trigger endorphin release."
      }
    ],
