    "title": "Cramps Before Your Period: Causes and What to Track",
    "description": "Premenstrual cramps are common and vary in timing and intensity. Learn what drives them, when they are normal, and which patterns warrant a provider visit.",
    "tags": [
      "guide"
    ],
    "publishedAt": "2026-04-28",
    "updatedAt": "2026-04-28",
    "buyerStage": "tofu",
    "targetPersona": [
      "flo-refugee",
      "post-roe-tracker",
      "paper-tracker",
      "privacy-conscious-user"
    ],
    "bluf": "Cramping before a period is caused by prostaglandins beginning to build in the uterine lining before menstruation starts. Most premenstrual cramps are normal and resolve once the period begins. Cramps that start far earlier in the cycle, are severe enough to limit daily function, or are accompanied by other symptoms may indicate an underlying condition like endometriosis.",
    "faqs": [
      {
        "q": "How early can pre-period cramps start?",
        "a": "Most commonly, cramps begin 1 to 2 days before the period. Some people notice mild cramping 3 to 5 days out. Cramping beginning more than a week before menstruation, particularly if it is significant, is outside the typical range for primary dysmenorrhea and worth investigating for secondary causes like endometriosis or adenomyosis."
      },
      {
        "q": "Can premenstrual cramps be on one side?",
        "a": "One-sided cramping before a period is possible and can be normal, but persistent one-sided pelvic pain warrants evaluation. Ovulation pain (mittelschmerz) is one-sided by nature and could be confused with premenstrual cramping if cycle timing is uncertain. Recurring, one-sided pain unrelated to ovulation timing may reflect a structural issue such as an ovarian cyst or endometrial implants."
      },
      {
        "q": "Do cramps before a period mean I am not pregnant?",
        "a": "Not necessarily. Early pregnancy can cause mild cramping as the uterus begins to change. The cramps of early pregnancy and premenstrual cramps can feel identical. A pregnancy test is the only way to distinguish between them if your period is late or you had unprotected sex this cycle."
      },
      {
        "q": "Can taking pain relief before the period starts help?",
        "a": "Anti-inflammatory medications like ibuprofen and naproxen work by reducing prostaglandin production. For people with primary dysmenorrhea, starting an NSAID a day before the period is expected (before prostaglandins build up significantly) can reduce cramping during the period. This strategy should be discussed with a healthcare provider, particularly if you take these medications regularly."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      {
        "question": "When do cramps before a period typically start?",
        "answer": "For most people with primary dysmenorrhea, cramps begin 1 to 2 days before menstruation starts and are most intense in the first day or two of flow. Some people start feeling dull lower abdominal heaviness or discomfort 3 to 5 days before their period. Cramps beginning significantly earlier, more than a week before the period, are less typical and worth tracking."
      },
      {
        "question": "Why do I get cramps before my period starts?",
        "answer": "As the luteal phase ends, the uterine lining begins producing prostaglandins in preparation for shedding. Prostaglandins cause the uterine muscle to contract. These contractions begin before bleeding actually starts, producing the cramping sensation. The same mechanism drives cramps during the period itself."
      },
      {
        "question": "Are cramps before a period different from cramps during a period?",
        "answer": "They are driven by the same mechanism (uterine contractions), but cramps before a period may feel more like a dull heaviness or ache, while cramps during the period can be sharper or more wave-like as the uterus works to expel the lining. Severity, character, and location can vary between the pre-period and menstrual phases for the same person."
      }
    ],
    "definitions": [
      {
        "term": "Prostaglandins",
        "definition": "Lipid compounds produced by the uterine lining that cause the uterus to contract. Higher levels of certain prostaglandins are associated with stronger menstrual cramps. Prostaglandin production increases in the late luteal phase before menstruation begins."
      },
      {
        "term": "Primary dysmenorrhea",
        "definition": "Painful periods caused by prostaglandin-driven uterine contractions rather than an underlying structural condition. The most common form of menstrual pain. Typically begins shortly before or at the start of menstruation and resolves within the first two days of flow."
      },
      {
        "term": "Secondary dysmenorrhea",
        "definition": "Painful periods caused by an underlying condition such as endometriosis, adenomyosis, fibroids, or pelvic inflammatory disease. Secondary dysmenorrhea often begins earlier in the cycle, lasts longer, and may worsen over time."
      },
      {
        "term": "Luteal phase",
        "definition": "The second half of the menstrual cycle, between ovulation and the next period. Premenstrual cramps typically occur in the late luteal phase, the final days before flow begins."
      },
      {
        "term": "Adenomyosis",
        "definition": "A condition in which uterine lining tissue grows into the muscular wall of the uterus. It often causes painful, heavy periods and cramping that can begin earlier in the cycle than typical primary dysmenorrhea."
      }
    ],
