    "title": "Discharge Before Your Period: What Each Type Means",
    "description": "Pre-period discharge is typically white and thick under progesterone influence. Learn what each type signals and when changes warrant medical attention.",
    "tags": [
      "guide"
    ],
    "publishedAt": "2026-04-28",
    "updatedAt": "2026-04-28",
    "buyerStage": "tofu",
    "targetPersona": [
      "flo-refugee",
      "post-roe-tracker",
      "paper-tracker",
      "privacy-conscious-user"
    ],
    "bluf": "Discharge in the days before your period is typically white or off-white, thicker than mid-cycle discharge, and odorless. This is normal luteal phase mucus produced under progesterone influence. Changes in color (yellow, green, gray), strong odor, or significant itching and irritation point to infection rather than normal cycle variation and should be evaluated by a clinician.",
    "faqs": [
      {
        "q": "Why is my discharge thick and white before my period but clearer at other times?",
        "a": "The change is hormone-driven. Around ovulation, estrogen is high and cervical mucus is produced in larger amounts, becoming clear and stretchy (often compared to raw egg whites) to facilitate sperm passage. After ovulation, progesterone causes mucus to thicken and become less permeable, producing the white, paste-like or lotion-like discharge typical of the luteal phase. This shift is a normal and predictable part of the cycle."
      },
      {
        "q": "Can yeast infections be more common before a period?",
        "a": "Yes. The vaginal environment shifts in the luteal phase as progesterone levels rise, and pH and glycogen levels change in ways that can favor Candida overgrowth. Some people find they are prone to yeast infections in the week before their period, particularly after antibiotic use, during high-stress periods, or when blood sugar is not well-regulated."
      },
      {
        "q": "How much discharge before a period is normal?",
        "a": "Discharge volume varies significantly between people and across cycles. There is no single amount that defines normal. What matters more than quantity is the quality: normal discharge is typically white to clear, lacks strong odor, and does not cause itching or irritation. Volume can increase around ovulation and in early pregnancy."
      },
      {
        "q": "Does discharge tracking help with fertility awareness?",
        "a": "Yes. Tracking the consistency and appearance of cervical mucus across the cycle is a component of fertility awareness methods. The shift from dry or sticky mucus to clear, stretchy mucus signals approaching ovulation. The return to thicker, white mucus after ovulation signals the luteal phase has begun. Over several cycles, this pattern can help identify the fertile window."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      {
        "question": "Is white discharge before my period normal?",
        "answer": "Yes, white or off-white discharge in the days before your period is a normal feature of the luteal phase. Under progesterone influence, cervical mucus becomes thicker and less watery than mid-cycle discharge. It is typically opaque, may appear in small to moderate amounts, and has little to no odor. This type of discharge does not indicate infection."
      },
      {
        "question": "What does discharge look like right before your period?",
        "answer": "In the day or two immediately before menstruation, discharge may shift from the thicker white of the luteal phase to slightly more watery or pink-tinged as estrogen and progesterone drop. Some people notice a small amount of pinkish or brownish spotting mixed with discharge as the uterine lining begins to shed. Clear, watery discharge right before a period is also common."
      },
      {
        "question": "What color of discharge should I be concerned about before my period?",
        "answer": "Yellow or green discharge, particularly if it has an unusual odor or is accompanied by itching or irritation, is not normal cycle variation and may indicate bacterial vaginosis, a yeast infection, or a sexually transmitted infection. Gray discharge with a fishy odor is associated with bacterial vaginosis. Any of these should be evaluated by a clinician."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can more discharge before my period indicate pregnancy?",
        "answer": "Some people notice increased vaginal discharge in early pregnancy due to rising estrogen and increased blood flow to the pelvic region. However, increased discharge alone is not a reliable pregnancy indicator. It also occurs in normal cycles and with infections. A pregnancy test is the appropriate way to check."
      }
    ],
    "definitions": [
      {
        "term": "Cervical mucus",
        "definition": "Fluid produced by glands in the cervix. Its consistency, volume, and appearance change across the menstrual cycle in response to estrogen and progesterone. Tracking cervical mucus is one method of fertility awareness."
      },
      {
        "term": "Leukorrhea",
        "definition": "Normal vaginal discharge, typically white or clear, of varying thickness, and without strong odor. It is produced throughout the cycle by the cervix and vaginal walls and is not a sign of infection."
      },
      {
        "term": "Bacterial vaginosis (BV)",
        "definition": "An imbalance in vaginal bacteria that produces a thin, grayish discharge with a characteristic fishy odor, often more noticeable after sex. BV is not a sexually transmitted infection but is associated with sexual activity. It requires antibiotic treatment."
      },
      {
        "term": "Candidiasis (yeast infection)",
        "definition": "An overgrowth of Candida fungus in the vagina causing thick, white, clumpy discharge (often described as cottage-cheese-like), itching, and irritation. Yeast infections are treated with antifungal medications and are more common in the late luteal phase when the vaginal environment shifts."
      },
      {
        "term": "Progesterone",
        "definition": "The dominant hormone of the luteal phase (after ovulation). Progesterone causes cervical mucus to become thicker and less permeable, which is why pre-period discharge tends to be white and paste-like compared to the clear, stretchy discharge around ovulation."
      }
    ],
