    "title": "Flo Anonymous Mode Explained: What It Actually Protects",
    "description": "Flo launched Anonymous Mode in 2022 after Dobbs. It removes your name from server data but doesn't change where data lives. Here's what it covers.",
    "tags": [
      "guide"
    ],
    "publishedAt": "2026-04-16",
    "updatedAt": "2026-04-16",
    "buyerStage": "tofu",
    "targetPersona": [
      "flo-refugee",
      "post-roe-tracker",
      "privacy-conscious-user"
    ],
    "bluf": "Anonymous Mode removes your name and email from cycle data Flo stores on its servers. It does not move the data off Flo's servers, does not cover data collected before you enabled it, and does not prevent re-identification. Flo paid a $59.5M class action settlement after Anonymous Mode launched. On-device storage solves the same problem structurally without asking you to trust an anonymization claim.",
    "faqs": [
      {
        "q": "When did Flo launch Anonymous Mode?",
        "a": "Flo launched Anonymous Mode in September 2022, three months after the Supreme Court's Dobbs decision overturned Roe v. Wade on June 24, 2022. The feature was positioned as a direct response to user concern about reproductive data being accessed by law enforcement."
      },
      {
        "q": "Does Anonymous Mode prevent Flo from selling data?",
        "a": "Flo's post-FTC privacy policy states the company does not sell user data. Anonymous Mode does not change that policy. What Anonymous Mode changes is whether cycle records on Flo's servers are stored alongside your name and email for users who opt in. Any restrictions on data sharing come from the FTC consent order and Flo's current policy, not from Anonymous Mode itself."
      },
      {
        "q": "Is Anonymous Mode free on Flo?",
        "a": "As of the FTC consent order period, Anonymous Mode was only available to Flo Premium subscribers. Flo has adjusted premium feature gating over time, so check the current Flo app for the active pricing. The point remains: Anonymous Mode is a product feature layered on top of cloud architecture, not a structural change to where data lives."
      },
      {
        "q": "Does Anonymous Mode protect data collected before I enabled it?",
        "a": "No. Anonymous Mode applies from the point you turn it on forward. Any cycle data, pregnancy data, symptoms, or behavioral data Flo already stored on its servers before Anonymous Mode was enabled is unaffected by the feature."
      },
      {
        "q": "Can Flo re-identify Anonymous Mode data?",
        "a": "Flo states that Anonymous Mode decouples cycle data from direct identifiers. Pseudonymized data is generally considered re-identifiable when combined with device IDs, IP addresses, or other logs. Flo has not published a technical architecture document that third parties can audit to verify what re-identification is or is not possible."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      {
        "question": "What is Flo Anonymous Mode?",
        "answer": "Anonymous Mode is a Flo feature launched in September 2022 that removes your name and email from the cycle data Flo stores on its servers. Flo describes it as a way to use the app without your health data being linked to your identity. It does not move data off Flo's servers, does not cover data collected before you turned it on, and does not prevent government agencies from subpoenaing the pseudonymized records."
      },
      {
        "question": "Does Flo Anonymous Mode protect against subpoenas?",
        "answer": "No. Anonymous Mode removes your name and email from cycle data, but the records still sit on Flo's servers. A subpoena or court order can compel Flo to produce the underlying records regardless of whether a name is attached. If the subpoena targets a specific device, IP address, account, or advertising ID, those identifiers can often be linked back to cycle data through other logs Flo holds."
      },
      {
        "question": "Is Flo Anonymous Mode actually anonymous?",
        "answer": "Not in the strict sense. Removing a name and email is pseudonymization, not anonymization. The data still exists, it still sits on company servers, and it can often be re-identified through other data points. JHU information security expert Anton Dahbura has noted that anonymous mode may lure people into a false sense of security."
      },
      {
        "question": "Did Anonymous Mode change anything about Flo's data practices?",
        "answer": "It changed what identifiers are stored alongside cycle data for users who opt in. It did not change the underlying architecture. Flo is still a cloud-based app. Your data still leaves your phone and lives on Flo's servers. The $59.5M class action settlement that closed in September 2025 covered conduct from 2016 to 2019, before Anonymous Mode existed, but the lawsuit itself was litigated through 2025 while Anonymous Mode was live."
      }
    ],
    "definitions": [
      {
        "term": "Anonymous Mode",
        "definition": "A Flo feature that removes a user's name and email from cycle data stored on Flo's servers. Data still lives on Flo's infrastructure. Launched in September 2022, three months after the Supreme Court's Dobbs decision."
      },
      {
        "term": "Pseudonymization",
        "definition": "Replacing direct identifiers like name or email with a pseudonym or random ID. Does not prevent re-identification when pseudonymized records can be combined with other data points like device ID, IP address, or behavioral patterns."
      },
      {
        "term": "Re-identification risk",
        "definition": "The probability that pseudonymized data can be traced back to a specific person using other data points (IP address, advertising ID, device fingerprint, app usage timing). Research on pseudonymized datasets consistently shows re-identification is possible with a small number of auxiliary data points."
      }
    ],
