    "title": "How to Switch from Clue to a Private Period Tracker",
    "description": "Export your Clue cycle history, delete the account under GDPR, file a formal data request, and move to a local-first tracker with less readable company-held data.",
    "tags": [
      "guide",
      "migration"
    ],
    "publishedAt": "2026-04-16",
    "updatedAt": "2026-04-16",
    "buyerStage": "mofu",
    "targetPersona": [
      "privacy-conscious-user",
      "post-roe-tracker"
    ],
    "bluf": "Clue has the cleanest record among major period trackers, but cloud storage still means subpoena risk. Here's how to export your cycle history and switch to an on-device app.",
    "faqs": [
      {
        "q": "Clue is GDPR-compliant, so why switch",
        "a": "GDPR compliance means Clue follows rules about how cloud data is handled. It does not change the fact that cloud data exists. A server holding cycle data can be subpoenaed, breached, or subjected to a policy change. An on-device tracker removes the server from the equation entirely, which is a stronger privacy posture than any compliance regime can offer."
      },
      {
        "q": "Can Clue data be subpoenaed for a reproductive-rights case in the US",
        "a": "Clue is Berlin-based, which provides some distance from US subpoenas, but not immunity. US courts can seek mutual legal assistance treaty (MLAT) cooperation for data held abroad, and Clue has stated it would challenge such requests. The challenge is not a guarantee. On-device tracking removes the subpoena target because there is no readable central copy to produce."
      },
      {
        "q": "Will I lose my period predictions when I switch",
        "a": "Temporarily. Every tracker needs 2-3 recorded cycles to calibrate its prediction algorithm. Seeding the new app with your last 3-6 cycle start dates from the Clue export minimizes the calibration gap. After two full cycles with the new app, predictions are usually back to baseline accuracy."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      {
        "question": "How do I switch from Clue without losing my cycle history",
        "answer": "Export the CSV from Clue (Settings, Data Management, Export Data) before deleting the account. Open the CSV in a spreadsheet to read your cycle start dates. Enter the last 3-6 start dates into your new tracker to seed the prediction algorithm."
      },
      {
        "question": "Does uninstalling Clue delete my data",
        "answer": "No. Uninstalling only removes the app from your device. Your data remains on Clue's servers until you delete the account and submit a deletion request."
      },
      {
        "question": "What's the best replacement for Clue",
        "answer": "Floriva, Euki, and Drip are the common picks. All three keep cycle data on-device rather than on a company server. Euki and Drip are free. Floriva is paid and focuses on polish. The right pick depends on budget and whether you want a nonprofit, open-source, or commercial maintainer behind the app."
      }
    ],
    "definitions": [
      {
        "term": "Account deletion vs. app deletion",
        "definition": "Deleting the app removes it from your phone. Deleting the account removes your data from the company's servers. These are different actions."
      },
      {
        "term": "CCPA/GDPR deletion request",
        "definition": "Legal right to request complete deletion of your personal data from a company. Applies in California (CCPA) and the EU/UK (GDPR)."
      }
    ],
