    "title": "Period Tracker Privacy Guide",
    "description": "A practical guide to evaluating period tracker privacy claims, spotting cloud-data risks, and choosing apps that keep reproductive data off company servers.",
    "tags": [
      "privacy",
      "period-tracking",
      "data-protection"
    ],
    "publishedAt": "2026-03-30",
    "updatedAt": "2026-04-02",
    "buyerStage": "tofu",
    "targetPersona": [
      "privacy-conscious",
      "post-dobbs-worried",
      "data-sensitive"
    ],
    "bluf": "Most period tracker apps collect far more data than they need, and their privacy policies give them permission to share it. This guide shows you exactly what to look for and what to avoid when choosing a period tracker that actually protects your data.",
    "faqs": [],
    "answers": [
      {
        "question": "What's the safest period tracker for privacy?",
        "answer": "A tracker that keeps core records on your device is the safest option for many people. If the app company has no readable cycle database, there is less for law enforcement to request and less to expose in a breach. Euki (nonprofit, local-only, free, iOS and Android) and Drip (Android, open source) are the most-referenced local-only options. Floriva uses local-first storage with optional encrypted sync."
      },
      {
        "question": "Does HIPAA protect period tracker apps?",
        "answer": "No. Period trackers are not healthcare providers or covered entities under HIPAA. A ClearDATA/Harris Poll survey (May 2023, n=2,053) found 81% of Americans wrongly believe HIPAA covers health apps. The HIPAA Reproductive Privacy Rule, which would have added some protections, was vacated by a federal court on June 18, 2025. There is currently no federal health data protection for period tracker apps."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can law enforcement access my Flo or Clue data?",
        "answer": "Yes. Both Flo and Clue store user data on their own servers. A valid court order or subpoena served on either company would require them to produce that data. The FTC's 2021 enforcement action documented that Flo shared user health data with Facebook and Google through embedded SDKs; a combined class action settlement against Flo, Google, and Flurry reached $59.5 million in September 2025 (as of original publication; check current rates)."
      }
    ],
    "definitions": [
      {
        "term": "Zero-knowledge storage",
        "definition": "A data storage model where the service provider holds only encrypted data they cannot read. The encryption keys never leave the user's device. Even if the company is subpoenaed, they can only produce ciphertext that is useless without the user's key."
      },
      {
        "term": "On-device storage",
        "definition": "A storage model where data is saved locally to the user's phone or tablet and never transmitted to a remote server. There is no company-held copy of the data, which means no subpoena surface and no breach exposure from company servers."
      },
      {
        "term": "Subpoena",
        "definition": "A legal order requiring a person or organization to produce documents or records. When law enforcement serves a subpoena on a tech company, the company must provide any data it holds for the specified user. Companies that store user data on their servers have data to produce; companies that do not hold user data have less company-held data to produce."
      }
    ],
