    "sources": [],
    "steps": [
      {
        "title": "Start logging now, even if your cycles are already irregular",
        "content": "Do not wait for your cycle to 'normalize' before tracking. Log today's date and note where you are in your current cycle if known. Irregular data tracked consistently is far more useful than waiting for a pattern that may not emerge on its own."
      },
      {
        "title": "Record cycle day 1 every time",
        "content": "Day 1 is the first day of full menstrual flow, not spotting. Mark this clearly. Over time, the dates between each day 1 give you cycle lengths. A set of cycle lengths (e.g., 28, 42, 31, 55, 29 days) is your baseline, even if it looks irregular."
      },
      {
        "title": "Track flow, spotting, and any unusual bleeding separately",
        "content": "Log flow intensity for each day of your period. Record any spotting or bleeding that occurs outside your expected period window. Mid-cycle spotting, post-coital bleeding, and intermenstrual bleeding are all distinct patterns with different clinical implications."
      },
      {
        "title": "Add symptom tracking for the full cycle",
        "content": "Irregular cycles are often driven by hormonal factors that cause symptoms throughout the month, not just during menstruation. Log energy, mood, acne, bloating, and any other symptoms daily. Patterns may correlate with hormonal fluctuations even if cycle timing is unpredictable."
      },
      {
        "title": "Calculate your range, not your average",
        "content": "With irregular cycles, an average cycle length is less useful than a range. If your cycles have run 25, 41, 30, and 62 days, your range is 25 to 62 days. This range defines your baseline and helps identify when a cycle is unusually long or short even within your irregular pattern."
      }
    ],
    "metaDescription": "Irregular cycles are harder to track but more important to document. What counts as irregular, how to build a useful baseline, and when to see a provider.",
