    "steps": [
      {
        "title": "Log mood daily on a simple scale",
        "content": "Use a 1-5 or 1-10 scale for overall mood, and separate scales or notes for anxiety, irritability, and depression. Log every day of the cycle, including the follicular phase (after period, before ovulation). The contrast between phases is what makes the luteal pattern visible."
      },
      {
        "title": "Note the first day your period starts each cycle",
        "content": "This anchors your cycle data. Counting backwards from the period start date lets you identify approximately which days fell in the luteal phase, even if you did not track ovulation directly."
      },
      {
        "title": "Track when symptoms resolve",
        "content": "For PMDD, the timing of symptom resolution is as diagnostically important as the timing of onset. Log the first day you feel noticeably better after your period starts. Resolution within 2 to 3 days of menstruation onset is characteristic of PMDD."
      },
      {
        "title": "Identify your typical luteal phase trigger window",
        "content": "After two to three cycles of daily logging, look for the day in your cycle when mood symptoms typically begin. For many people with PMDD, this is around 7 to 14 days before menstruation. Knowing your personal trigger window allows for planning and support."
      }
    ],
